  "version": 1,
  "_comment": "Track guides. Served from the content repo so circuits and corrections ship without an App Store submission. Every field except id/name/matchKeys is optional - the app renders only what is present and never invents missing data. trackPath is SVG path data in viewBox units; replace the stylised seeds with accurate outlines (e.g. from Wikimedia Commons circuit SVGs) whenever available. image is an optional photograph for the race header and the Home screen's Next Race card: freely-licensed sources only (Wikimedia Commons, Flickr CC, public domain), never agency or press photography. credit is a licence condition, not a courtesy - the app hides any image whose credit is missing, so a half-filled entry disappears rather than shipping unattributed.",
  "circuits": [
    {
      "id": "silverstone",
      "name": "Silverstone Circuit",
      "location": "Silverstone, Northamptonshire, United Kingdom",
      "country": "United Kingdom",
      "matchKeys": ["silverstone", "british grand prix", "great britain"],
      "image": {
        "url": "images/circuits/silverstone.jpg",
        "credit": "Planet Labs, Inc.",
        "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "licenceURL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
        "sourceURL": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silverstone_Circuit,_July_2,_2018_SkySat.jpg"
      },
      "lengthKm": 5.891,
      "corners": 18,
      "firstHeld": 1948,
      "lapRecord": {
        "time": "1:27.097",
        "holder": "Max Verstappen",
        "year": 2020,
        "series": "Formula 1"
      },
      "overview": "Built on a former RAF airfield in 1948, Silverstone is one of the fastest circuits in world motorsport. Its flowing high-speed sequences reward commitment above all else, and the Maggotts-Becketts-Chapel complex is widely regarded as the finest test of an aerodynamic car anywhere on the calendar.",
      "facts": [
        "Silverstone hosted the very first Formula 1 World Championship race on 13 May 1950.",
        "The circuit started life as a Royal Air Force bomber station, and the original layout ran along the perimeter roads and runways.",
        "The layout has been reconfigured several times, so lap records from the 1950s, 1970s and 1990s are not directly comparable with today's times.",
        "The current Grand Prix configuration has been in use since 2011."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "zandvoort",
      "name": "Circuit Zandvoort",
      "location": "Zandvoort, North Holland, Netherlands",
      "country": "Netherlands",
      "matchKeys": ["zandvoort", "dutch grand prix"],
      "image": {
        "url": "images/circuits/zandvoort.jpg",
        "credit": "dronepicr",
        "licence": "CC BY 2.0",
        "licenceURL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
        "sourceURL": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luftbild_der_Circuit_Park_Zandvoort_Rennstrecke_Formel_1_(46940306055).jpg"
      },
      "lengthKm": 4.259,
      "corners": 14,
      "firstHeld": 1948,
      "overview": "Laid out among the North Sea dunes and designed by John Hugenholtz, Zandvoort is narrow, undulating and unforgiving. A 2020 modernisation added banking to two corners to help cars follow each other more closely, giving the circuit a character found nowhere else on the Formula 1 calendar.",
      "facts": [
        "Zandvoort is the only circuit on the Formula 1 calendar with banked corners.",
        "The Hugenholtzbocht at Turn 3 is banked at 18 percent - roughly 32 degrees.",
        "Turn 14, the Arie Luyendykbocht, is named after the two-time Indianapolis 500 winner and is also banked.",
        "The Dutch Grand Prix ran here from 1952 to 1985 before the circuit returned to Formula 1 in 2021."
      ]
    },
